<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kilij Arslan II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1192">1192</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kilij Arslan II was sultan of the Seljuk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a> from 1156 to 1192. He annexed the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danishmendids">Danishmendids</a> in the 1170s and destroyed a Byzantine army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel I Komnenos</a> at Myriokephalon in 1176, ending Byzantine hopes of recovering central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. In old age he divided his realm among his sons, which led to civil war.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-ii.jpg" alt="Kilij Arslan II" width="320" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 1156-92 artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sultan_Kilij_Arslan_II_(portrait)_in_a_tile_from_Alaeddin_Palace,_Konya,_1156-92.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kilij Arslan II was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesud-i">Mesud I</a>, sultan of Rum. The Byzantine historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niketas-choniates">Niketas Choniates</a> described him as maimed in several vital parts of his body, with dislocated hands and a slight limp, so that he travelled mostly in a litter. This was the origin of the Byzantine nickname Koutz-Arslan, &quot;Halt-Arslan&quot;. In 1148 he captured the city of Marash, after the surrounding region had been devastated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danishmendids">Danishmendids</a> in 1136-1137.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In early 1159 the sultan's forces harassed the rear-guard of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel I Komnenos</a> near Kotyaion after Kilij Arslan had refused the emperor passage, which began the Byzantine-Seljuk War of 1158-1162. Kilij Arslan then travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in late 1160 or 1161, swore oaths of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a>, promised to return to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> any notable cities he conquered, and was ritually adopted as a son by Manuel.</p><p>After the Danishmendid emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sebasteia</a>, Yaghibasan, died in autumn 1164, Kilij attacked Sebasteia without success but subjugated most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>'s lands in 1165, including Albistan, Darende and Geduk. He did not return them to Byzantine control. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a>'s death in 1174 he occupied Sebasteia, Neokaisareia, Doceia, Comana and other towns in Cappadocia in early 1175.</p><p>In 1175 Kilij refused to hand the conquered territory to Manuel, who invaded in 1176 intending to capture Iconium. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-myriokephalon">Battle of Myriokephalon</a> the sultan defeated Manuel's army and forced the emperor to negotiate a fragile peace. In 1179 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-iii">Pope Alexander III</a> wrote to him, and Otto of Sankt Blasien reports that he sought a marriage with a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>. His overtures to pope and emperor were probably less about religion than about balancing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> through warm relations with the west.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Manuel's death in 1180 Kilij secured most of the southern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. In 1186 he divided the sultanate among his eleven sons, who began to fight one another for control. When the armies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> crossed his lands, his sons fought them at Philomelion and Iconium, although he had promised free passage. His eldest son Qutb al-Din took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>, and Kilij took refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayseri">Kayseri</a>. In 1192 Kilij and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaykhusraw-i">Kaykhusraw I</a> drove Qutb al-Din out of Konya and besieged him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksaray">Aksaray</a>. Kilij died during the siege in August 1192, after promising Kaykhusraw I the succession, and was buried in the Alâeddin Mosque in Konya.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kilij Arslan II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1176 <b>Kilij Arslan II</b> trapped and defeated the army of the Byzantine emperor Manuel I Komnenos at the Battle of Myriokephalon, ending Byzantine hopes of recovering central Anatolia; two years later he absorbed the rival Danishmendid emirate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Seljuk sultan <b>Kilij Arslan II</b> ambushed the army of the Byzantine emperor Manuel I Komnenos in a narrow pass as it marched on Konya.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-17">17 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Kilij Arslan II</b> of the Sultanate of Rum annexed Sivas around 1174 and the Malatya branch in 1178, ending the dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/danishmendids">Danishmendids</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.85,32.52&amp;year=1182" aria-label="Konya on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5361 803 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Kilij Arslan II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5811 1073h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5811 1073h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.85,32.52&amp;year=1182">What was here in 1182? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
